overflowed its stack

Blocker: chelis#2307 -- Std.Io.Json parses with depth proportional to the
element count, so the parser exhausts the stack before Coral sees the document.
Bisected to adjacent integers at the 0.19.1 pin, macOS arm64: the bare parser,
with no Coral symbol on the path, passes 972 array elements and fails at 973 on
this worker lane, and read_json_frame itself passes 971 and fails at 972. The
one-element gap is a constant: Coral costs a handful of frames, not a
per-element cost, so the bound is the parser's while the absolute figure is one
element lower through Coral. On the chelis eval lane both pass 236 and fail at
237. An array of bare scalars and one of objects agree to within one element.

Fixed upstream by chelis#3336, merged after v0.19.1 and therefore not in the
pinned toolchain.

Known weakness of this probe: line 1 above matches ANY stack overflow in this
file, including a Coral-owned one, because the worker-kill diagnostic names no
component. It can therefore report "still blocked" for the wrong reason. The
2,000-row document is roughly twice the measured bound, so a partial upstream
improvement could also leave it blocked. Read the bisected figures above rather
than this probe's pass as the statement of where the bound is.

De-narrowing: when this probe reports FIX-detected at a pin bump, promote it to
tests/io.ch, raise the row count to match the CSV paths, and archive the
docs/UPSTREAM_BUGS.md entry.
